3	//! Grant-table client — the granting half of `SYS_SAFECOPY` (slice 5.2, D4).
4	//!
5	//! A server that wants to hand a peer access to some memory keeps a
6	//! [`GrantPool`] and calls [`grant_direct`](GrantPool::grant_direct) (its own
7	//! memory) or [`grant_magic`](GrantPool::grant_magic) (a third party's, for a
8	//! server-grade granter). The returned grant id travels to the grantee in an
9	//! ordinary message payload; the grantee names it in `SYS_SAFECOPY` and the
10	//! kernel reads the entry back out of *this* address space to validate it.
11	//!
12	//! ## The pool is a value, not a static
13	//!
14	//! MINIX's `cpf_*` functions work on a process-global table. Here the pool is
15	//! an ordinary value the server owns — typically a local in `main` that lives
16	//! as long as the receive loop. That is what keeps `server-rt`
17	//! `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`: there is no static mutable state to reach through
18	//! an `UnsafeCell`, and no `unsafe impl Sync`.
19	//!
20	//! It also makes registration **self-healing**. The kernel is told one address;
21	//! if the pool were ever moved, that address would name freed stack.
22	//! [`ensure_registered`](GrantPool::ensure_registered) compares the pool's
23	//! current address against the one last registered and re-issues `SYS_SETGRANT`
24	//! when they differ — taking a pointer and casting it to an integer are both
25	//! safe operations, so this costs no `unsafe`.
26	//!
27	//! ## Sizing
28	//!
29	//! A server's stack is one page, and the pool is `N * 32` bytes plus two words.
30	//! Keep `N` small — the slice-5.2 demo uses 8 (256 bytes). `N` must also fit
31	//! the grant id's index field; an over-large pool is rejected by the kernel at
32	//! registration (`EINVAL`) rather than silently truncated.
33	//!
34	//! Only [`ensure_registered`](GrantPool::ensure_registered) traps into the
35	//! kernel; the allocation, sequence-bump, and revoke logic are pure helpers over
36	//! a borrowed slice and carry the host unit tests, the
37	//! `servers/ds/src/registry.rs` shape.
38	
39	use minixrs_ipc::ipc_sendrec;
40	use minixrs_kernel_shared::Message;
41	use minixrs_kernel_shared::callnr::SYS_SETGRANT;
42	use minixrs_kernel_shared::com::{SYSTEM, boot_endpoint};
43	use minixrs_kernel_shared::endpoint::{Endpoint, NONE};
44	use minixrs_kernel_shared::error::{EINVAL, ENOMEM, OK};
45	use minixrs_kernel_shared::grant::{
46	    CPF_ACCESS_MASK, CPF_DIRECT, CPF_MAGIC, CPF_USED, CPF_VALID, GRANT_MAX_SEQ, GrantEntry,
47	    grant_id, grant_idx, grant_seq, grant_valid,
48	};
